<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Introduction # {#introduction}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

*This section is non-normative.*

Today, websites that support federated sign-in pick a small number of large [=IDPs=] to show in
their sign-in flows, because each one takes space and needs a separate integration. Users whose
identity lives elsewhere (on a smaller provider, a custom domain, or a server they run themselves)
are left out. By contrast, email verification lets any mail server take part without the website
allow-listing it.

This specification lets the [=user agent=] act as an intermediary that removes the need for
allow-listing:

1. After the user signs in, an [=IDP=] **registers** the user's [=/handle=] by calling
    {{IdentityProvider/register()}}, with the user's permission.
1. An [=RP=] **requests** any registered [=IDP=] that supports a federation it accepts, by passing a
    {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} instead of a {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}}:

    <div class="example" id="example-rp-request">
    ```js
    const credential = await navigator.credentials.get({
      identity: {
        providers: [{
          federation: "https://www.w3.org/TR/indieauth/",
          params: { nonce: "..." }
        }, {
          configURL: "https://idp.example/fedcm.json",
          clientId: "123"
        }]
      }
    });
    ```

    </div>

1. A user whose [=/handle=] isn't registered yet can **type it**, for example `@alice.example`,
    `alice@social.example` or `https://alice.example`. The user agent
    [=look up a typed handle|resolves it=] to its [=issuer=] and registers it
    ([=find your account=]).

Both ways of registering a [=/handle=] run the same [[#resolution|resolution]] and add the same
[=/handle=] to the [=handle registry=].

<div class="example" id="example-idp-register">
After Alice signs in on `https://idp.example`, the [=IDP=] sets its login status and registers her
handle:

```js
await navigator.login.setStatus("logged-in");
await IdentityProvider.register("@alice.idp.example");
```

The user agent resolves `@alice.idp.example` to `idp.example`'s [=config file=], which lists the
federations it supports:

```json
{
  "accounts_endpoint": "/accounts",
  "id_assertion_endpoint": "/assertion",
  "login_url": "/login",
  "federations": ["https://www.w3.org/TR/indieauth/", "https://atproto.com"]
}
```

And its {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} includes her [=/handle=] on her account:

```json
{
  "accounts": [{
    "id": "1234",
    "name": "Alice",
    "handle": "@alice.idp.example",
    "picture": "https://idp.example/alice.jpg"
  }]
}
```

</div>

This specification is written as a set of monkey patches to [[FEDCM]], shown like this:

<div class="monkeypatch">
A change to an algorithm or definition in another specification.

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Handles # {#handles}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

This specification depends on the Infra Standard. [[!INFRA]]

A <dfn>handle</dfn> is a globally-unique and human-readable [=string=] that identifies the user,
that is produced by [=parse a handle=], and that is resolvable by
[=resolve a handle|Resolution=]. A handle isn't necessarily permanent: a user can change it, or
move it to another [=IDP=].

An <dfn>issuer</dfn> is an [=IDP=] that is the authority for a [=/handle=]. The user agent identifies an
issuer by its [=site of a host|site=], where it serves its [=well-known file=]. Each [=/handle=] has
exactly one issuer at a time, which [=resolve a handle|Resolution=] finds.

The <dfn>site of a host</dfn> |host| is |host|'s [=host/registrable domain=]. A [=host=] |host| is
a <dfn>site host</dfn> if |host| is a [=/domain=], its [=host/registrable domain=] is non-null, and
|host| equals its [=host/registrable domain=].

<div class="example" id="example-site-of-host">
The [=site of a host|site=] of `alice.social.example` is `social.example`, as long as
`social.example` isn't a [=host/public suffix=]. If `hosting.example` is a public suffix, the
[=site of a host|site=] of `alice.hosting.example` is `alice.hosting.example`.

</div>

This section defines how the user agent parses a [=/handle=], resolves it to its [=issuer=], and
stores the [=/handle|handles=] the user uses. None of this requires the user agent to understand
any identity protocol. It only needs a small set of handle notations ([[#parsing]]) and a single,
protocol-agnostic resolution procedure ([[#resolution]]).

## Parsing ## {#parsing}

[=Parse a handle=] accepts the following notations:

<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th>Notation<th>Examples<th>Used by
<tbody>
<tr><td>`@` + host<td>`@alice.example`, `@alice.social.example`<td>atproto
<tr><td>local + `@` + host, optionally with a leading `@`<td>`alice@social.example`,
    `@alice@social.example`<td>ActivityPub, Nostr (NIP-05)
<tr><td>`https:` URL<td>`https://alice.example/`<td>IndieAuth, Solid, OpenID 2.0
</table>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>parse a handle</dfn> given a [=string=] |input|, run the following steps. This returns a
[=/handle=] or failure.

1. Let |s| be |input| with leading and trailing [=ASCII whitespace=] [=strip leading and
    trailing ASCII whitespace|stripped=].
1. If |s| starts with "`https://`":
    1. Let |url| be the result of running the [=URL parser=] on |s|.
    1. If |url| is failure, return failure.
    1. Let |host| be |url|'s [=url/host=].
    1. Let |handle| be the result of running the [=URL serializer=] on |url|.
1. Otherwise:
    1. If |s| starts with "`@`", remove the first code point from |s|.
    1. If |s| contains "`@`":
        1. Let |local| be the part of |s| before the last "`@`", and |hostString| the part after it.
        1. If |local| is the empty string, return failure.
    1. Otherwise, let |local| be null and |hostString| be |s|.
    1. Let |host| be the result of running the [=host parser=] on |hostString|.
    1. If |host| is failure, return failure.
    1. If |local| is null, let |handle| be "`@`" followed by the [=host serializer|serialization=]
        of |host|. Otherwise, let |handle| be |local|, followed by "`@`", followed by the
        [=host serializer|serialization=] of |host|.
1. If |host| is not a [=/domain=], return failure.
1. If |host|'s [=host/registrable domain=] is null, return failure.
1. Return |handle|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn for="handle">site</dfn> of a [=/handle=] |handle| is the result of the following steps:

1. Let |host| be the [=url/host=] of the result of running the [=URL parser=] on |handle| if
    |handle| starts with "`https://`", and the result of running the [=host parser=] on the
    part of |handle| after the last "`@`" otherwise.
1. Return |host|'s [=host/registrable domain=].

</div>

Note: Because a [=/handle=] is always in the canonical form that [=parse a handle=] produces, its
[=handle/site=] is always a [=site host=].

<div class="example" id="example-parse-a-handle">
<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th>Input<th>Handle<th>Its [=handle/site=]
<tbody>
<tr><td>`@alice.example`, `alice.example`<td>`@alice.example`<td>`alice.example`
<tr><td>`@alice.social.example`<td>`@alice.social.example`<td>`social.example`
<tr><td>`alice@social.example`, `@alice@social.example`<td>`alice@social.example`<td>`social.example`
<tr><td>`https://alice.example`<td>`https://alice.example/`<td>`alice.example`
<tr><td>`@192.0.2.1`, `@com`<td colspan=2>failure
</table>
</div>

<div algorithm>
Two [=strings=] |a| and |b| <dfn lt="handles match">match as handles</dfn> if the following steps
return true:

1. If |a| and |b| both start with "`https://`":
    1. Let |urlA| and |urlB| be the results of running the [=URL parser=] on |a| and |b|.
    1. If either is failure, return false.
    1. Return whether |urlA| [=url/equals=] |urlB|.
1. Let |a'| and |b'| be the [=ASCII lowercase|ASCII lowercased=] |a| and |b|.
1. If |a'| starts with "`@`", remove its first code point. Do the same for |b'|.
1. Return whether |a'| is |b'|.

</div>

Note: This is the only comparison used on handles. It is applied to an account's
{{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}}, and never to its {{IdentityProviderAccount/username}} or
{{IdentityProviderAccount/email}}, so that this mechanism stays separate from how an [=IDP=]
displays accounts and from email verification.

Issue: URL handles need more normalization (fragments such as `#me`, trailing slashes).

## Resolution ## {#resolution}

<dfn lt="resolve a handle">Resolution</dfn> turns a [=/handle=] into the
[=config file|config URL=] of its [=issuer=].

<div algorithm="resolve a handle">
To [=resolve a handle=] given a [=/handle=] |handle| and a |globalObject|, run the following steps.
This returns a [=/URL=] or failure.

1. Let |issuer| be the result of [=resolve the issuer=] of |handle|. If |issuer| is failure,
    return failure.
1. Let |wellKnown| be the result of [=fetch the well-known file=] with |issuer| and |globalObject|.
    If |wellKnown| is failure, return failure.
1. If |wellKnown|["{{IdentityProviderWellKnown/provider_urls}}"] doesn't [=map/exist=] or its
    [=list/size=] is not 1, return failure.
1. Let |url| be the result of running the [=URL parser=] on
    |wellKnown|["{{IdentityProviderWellKnown/provider_urls}}"][0].
1. If |url| is failure, or |url|'s [=url/scheme=] is not "`https`", or |url|'s [=url/host=]'s
    [=host/registrable domain=] is not |issuer|, return failure.
1. Return |url|.

</div>

Resolution takes a [=/handle=], not arbitrary input. Callers that start from user or script input
first run [=parse a handle=], which also normalizes it (for example, `Alice.Example` becomes
`@alice.example`), and then resolve the result. Resolution stops at the config URL: it doesn't
fetch the [=config file=] or check its {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/federations}}, because each caller needs a different check.

Issue: Because [=resolve a handle|resolution=] requires exactly one
{{IdentityProviderWellKnown/provider_urls}} entry, a [=site of a host|site=] can register
[=/handle|handles=] for only one [=config file=]. Is that acceptable for [=IDPs=] that have several
configurations (e.g. one per product)?

<div class="monkeypatch">
Add the following algorithm:

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>fetch the well-known file</dfn> given a [=site host=] |site| and a |globalObject|, run the
steps of [=fetch the config file=] that create <var ignore>wellKnownRequest</var> and
[=fetch request|fetch=] it, with <var ignore>rootUrl</var> set to a new [=/URL=] with
[=url/scheme=] "`https`", [=url/host=] |site|, and [=url/path=] « "`.well-known`",
"`web-identity`" », and with |globalObject|. Return the resulting <var ignore>wellKnown</var>, an
{{IdentityProviderWellKnown}} or failure.

</div>

Then, in [=fetch the config file=], replace those steps with a call to [=fetch the well-known file=]
with <var ignore>rootUrl</var>'s [=url/host=] and |globalObject|.

Note: This only moves existing steps, so that [=resolve a handle|resolution=] can reuse them. It
doesn't change the behavior of [=fetch the config file=].

</div>

### Resolve the issuer ### {#resolve-issuer}

The first step of [=resolve a handle|resolution=] finds a [=/handle=]'s [=issuer=] by looking up a
[[#dns-record|delegation record]] on the handle's [=handle/site=]. Its `iss=` value names the
[=issuer=]'s site, whether the [=handle/site=] hosts its own [=IDP=] or delegates to another site.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>resolve the issuer</dfn> of a [=/handle=] |handle|, run the following steps. This returns
the [=site host=] of |handle|'s [=issuer=], or failure.

1. Let |records| be the result of [=look up the delegation records=] for |handle|'s
    [=handle/site=].
1. If |records| is failure or its [=list/size=] is not 1, return failure.
1. Let |value| be |records|[0] with the leading "`iss=`" removed.
1. Let |issuer| be the result of running the [=host parser=] on the
    [=ASCII lowercase|ASCII lowercased=] |value|.
1. If |issuer| is failure or is not a [=site host=], return failure.
1. Return |issuer|.

</div>

Note: When a provider's domain is a [=host/public suffix=] (for example, `hosting.example`, where each
user gets their own subdomain), each subdomain is its own [=handle/site=]. The [=handle/site=] of
`@alice.hosting.example` is `alice.hosting.example`, not `hosting.example`.

<div class="example" id="example-resolve-issuer">
<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th>Handle<th>Its [=handle/site=]<th>Delegation record<th>[=Issuer=]
<tbody>
<tr><td>`@alice.example`<td>`alice.example`<td>`iss=alice.example`<td>`alice.example`
<tr><td>`@alice.example`<td>`alice.example`<td>`iss=social.example`<td>`social.example`
<tr><td>`@alice.social.example`<td>`social.example`<td>`iss=social.example`<td>`social.example`
<tr><td>`alice@social.example`<td>`social.example`<td>`iss=social.example`<td>`social.example`
<tr><td>`https://alice.example/`<td>`alice.example`<td>`iss=alice.example`<td>`alice.example`
<tr><td>`@alice.hosting.example` (`hosting.example` is a public suffix)<td>`alice.hosting.example`<td>`iss=alice.hosting.example`<td>`alice.hosting.example`
<tr><td>`@alice.example`<td>`alice.example`<td>none<td>failure
<tr><td>`@alice.example`<td>`alice.example`<td>`iss=pds.social.example`<td>failure (not a site)
</table>
</div>

### DNS delegation record ### {#dns-record}

A [=/handle=]'s [=handle/site=] names its [=issuer=] by publishing a DNS `TXT` record [[!RFC1035]]
at the name `_web-identity.` followed by the [=handle/site=]:

<div class="example" id="example-dns-record">
```
_web-identity.alice.example.   TXT   "iss=social.example"
```

This record says that the [=issuer=] of handles whose [=handle/site=] is `alice.example` is on the
[=site of a host|site=] `social.example`. If `alice.example` is its own [=issuer=], the record is:

```
_web-identity.alice.example.   TXT   "iss=alice.example"
```

</div>

The record's value MUST be the string "`iss=`" followed by a [=site host=]. There MUST be
exactly one such record at a name.

Note: This is modeled on the DNS delegation in the
[Email Verification Protocol](https://dickhardt.github.io/email-verification/draft-hardt-email-verification.html#name-dns-delegation).
Unlike that record, the value here names a site rather than a host, so that resolution always
ends at a site's [=well-known file=].

Issue: Should the record name be `_web-identity` or something more specific (e.g. `_web-handle`)?
Should it carry a `federation`, so that one domain can delegate different protocols to different
[=IDPs=]?

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>look up the delegation records</dfn> for a [=site host=] |site|, the user agent performs a
DNS query for `TXT` records at the name `_web-identity.` followed by |site|, using its DNS
resolver. This returns a [=list=] of [=strings=] (the records whose contents start with "`iss=`"),
or failure if the query failed.

</div>

## The Handle Registry ## {#registry}

Each [=user agent=] has a global, persistent <dfn>handle registry</dfn>, an initially empty
[=ordered set=] of [=/handle|handles=] that the user uses to sign in. No two
[=/handle|handles=] in the [=handle registry=] [=handles match|match=].

A [=/handle=] is added to the [=handle registry=] in the same way whether the [=IDP=] registered it
with {{IdentityProvider/register()}} or the user typed it ([=find your account=]). The user agent
doesn't record which one did.

The [=handle registry=] stores only [=/handle|handles=]. It doesn't store which [=IDP=] a
[=/handle=] resolves to, that [=IDP=]'s [=config file=], or any accounts. The user agent computes
the [=IDP=] from the [=/handle=] by [=resolve a handle|resolving=] it whenever it needs it, and
obtains accounts from the [=IDP=] when needed.

Note: A user agent can cache the results of [=resolve a handle|resolution=] and [=fetch the config file=],
for example to avoid DNS lookups and fetches on every [=federation request=]. That's an implementation
choice, as long as the cache respects DNS TTLs and HTTP caching, and is cleared along with the
[=handle registry=]. See also [[#privacy]] and [[#security]].

When the user clears browsing data or site settings for an [=/origin=] |origin|, the user agent
MUST [=list/remove=] from the [=handle registry=] every [=/handle=] whose [=issuer=], as determined
by [=resolve the issuer=] (or by the user agent's cached result of it), is on the same
[=site of a host|site=] as |origin|.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>add to the handle registry</dfn> given a [=/handle=] |handle|, run the following steps:

1. If the [=handle registry=] contains a [=/handle=] that [=handles match|matches=] |handle|,
    return.
1. [=set/Append=] |handle| to the [=handle registry=].

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>remove from the handle registry</dfn> given a [=/handle=] |handle|, [=list/remove=] from
the [=handle registry=] every [=/handle=] that [=handles match|matches=] |handle|.

</div>

The user agent SHOULD let the user see the [=handle registry=] and remove [=/handle|handles=] from
it (by running [=remove from the handle registry=]), for example to remove a mistyped handle.

Note: The [=handle registry=] is deliberately separate from the [[LOGIN-STATUS|login status]] (and
from the [[ACCOUNT-REGISTRY|account registry]], when supported). The login status records whether
the user is signed in right now, which changes silently, while the [=handle registry=] records
which [=/handle|handles=] the user uses, which the user approved and which outlives their session.
After the user signs out, a registered [=/handle=] is still offered as a [=handle chip=] that signs
the user back in. So an [=IDP=] calls `setStatus("logged-out")` when the user signs out, and
{{IdentityProvider/unregister()}} only when the [=/handle=] should no longer be offered, for
example when the account is deleted.

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# IdP-initiated Registration # {#registration}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

This section defines how an [=IDP=] registers and unregisters a user's [=/handle=], and what it
declares so that the user agent can present it to [=RPs=] later ([[#presentation]]).

## Registering a handle ## {#register}

<xmp class="idl">
partial interface IdentityProvider {
  static Promise<undefined> register(USVString handle);
  static Promise<undefined> unregister(USVString handle);
};
</xmp>

An [=IDP=] registering a [=/handle=] and the user typing it ([[#user-registration]]) follow the
same path: both [=resolve a handle|resolve=] it, and both add the same [=/handle=] to the
[=handle registry=].

Note: An [=IDP=] can only register and unregister [=/handle|handles=] that it is the [=issuer=] of,
as named by the [[#dns-record|delegation record]] on the handle's [=handle/site=].

<div algorithm="register">
When the static <dfn method for="IdentityProvider">register(|handle|)</dfn> method is invoked, run
the following steps:

1. Let |globalObject| be the [=current global object=].
1. Let |promise| be a [=new=] {{Promise}} in |globalObject|'s [=relevant realm=].
1. If |globalObject|'s [=Window/navigable=] is not a [=top-level traversable=], [=reject=] |promise|
    with a "{{NotAllowedError}}" {{DOMException}} and return |promise|.
1. [=In parallel=], run the following steps:
    1. Let |canonical| be the result of [=parse a handle=] with |handle|. If |canonical| is
        failure, [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{SyntaxError}}" and return.
    1. Let |configUrl| be the result of [=resolve a handle=] with |canonical| and |globalObject|.
        If |configUrl| is failure, [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{NetworkError}}" and return.
    1. If |configUrl|'s [=url/origin=] is not [=same site=] with |globalObject|'s
        [=associated Document=]'s [=Document/origin=], [=queue to reject=] |promise| with
        "{{NotAllowedError}}" and return.
    1. Let |provider| be the result of [=create a registered provider=] with |configUrl| and null.
    1. Let |config| be the result of [=fetch the config file=] with |provider| and |globalObject|.
    1. If |config| is failure, or |config|.{{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/federations}} doesn't
        [=map/exist=] or [=list/is empty=], [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{NetworkError}}"
        and return.
    1. Let |accounts| be the result of [=get the registered accounts=] with |config|, |provider|,
        « |canonical| », and |globalObject|.
    1. If |accounts| [=list/is empty=], [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{NotAllowedError}}"
        and return.

        Note: This fails if the user isn't [=logged-in=] to the [=IDP=], if fetching
        {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} fails, or if none of the returned accounts
        has |canonical| as its {{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}}.
    1. If the [=handle registry=] doesn't already contain a [=/handle=] that
        [=handles match|matches=] |canonical|, the user agent MUST
        [=ask to save a handle|ask the user to save=] |canonical| with |accounts|[0] and |config|.
        If the user declines, [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{NotAllowedError}}" and return.
    1. [=Add to the handle registry=] with |canonical|.
    1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=DOM manipulation task source=] given |globalObject| to
        [=resolve=] |promise|.
1. Return |promise|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>ask to save a handle</dfn> given a [=/handle=] |handle|, an {{IdentityProviderAccount}}
|account|, and an {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig}} |config|, the user agent shows a prompt that asks the user whether
to save |handle| so that it's offered when websites ask them to sign in. It returns whether the
user agreed.

* The prompt SHOULD show |handle| as the account's identifier.
* The prompt SHOULD show |account|'s {{IdentityProviderAccount/name}} and
    {{IdentityProviderAccount/picture}}.
* The prompt SHOULD show |config|'s {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/branding}}, for example as a badge
    on the account's picture.
* The user agent MAY require [=transient activation=] before showing the prompt, MAY rate-limit
    it, and MAY remember that the user declined and not ask again.

</div>

Note: An [=IDP=] sets its login status to [=logged-in=] before calling
{{IdentityProvider/register()}}, as in the <a href="#example-idp-register">introduction's
example</a>. This way, an [=IDP=] can only register a [=/handle=] for an account the user is
signed in to, and the prompt can show that account's name and picture.

Note: {{IdentityProvider/register()}} is separate from {{NavigatorLogin/setStatus()}} because a
registered [=/handle=] needs to outlive the user's session. See [[#registry]].

<div algorithm="unregister">
When the static <dfn method for="IdentityProvider">unregister(|handle|)</dfn> method is invoked,
run the following steps:

1. Let |globalObject| be the [=current global object=].
1. Let |promise| be a [=new=] {{Promise}} in |globalObject|'s [=relevant realm=].
1. [=In parallel=], run the following steps:
    1. Let |canonical| be the result of [=parse a handle=] with |handle|. If |canonical| is
        failure, [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{SyntaxError}}" and return.
    1. Let |configUrl| be the result of [=resolve a handle=] with |canonical| and |globalObject|.
        If |configUrl| is failure, [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{NetworkError}}" and return.
    1. If |configUrl|'s [=url/origin=] is not [=same site=] with |globalObject|'s
        [=associated Document=]'s [=Document/origin=], [=queue to reject=] |promise| with
        "{{NotAllowedError}}" and return.
    1. [=Remove from the handle registry=] with |canonical|.
    1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=DOM manipulation task source=] given |globalObject| to
        [=resolve=] |promise|.
1. Return |promise|.

</div>

{{IdentityProvider/unregister()}} removes the [=/handle=] whether the [=IDP=] registered it or the
user typed it: either way, the handle's [=issuer=] has said it should no longer be offered. It resolves
the same way whether or not the [=/handle=] was in the [=handle registry=], so that it doesn't
reveal whether the user had registered it.

Note: If a [=/handle=] no longer resolves to the caller, for example because its
[[#dns-record|delegation record]] was removed, the caller can't unregister it. It doesn't need to,
because the [=/handle=] is no longer offered with the caller as its [=issuer=] either
([=gather registered providers=] resolves it again).

## Account handles ## {#account-handles}

<xmp class="idl">
partial dictionary IdentityProviderAccount {
  USVString handle;
};
</xmp>

<dl dfn-type="dict-member" dfn-for="IdentityProviderAccount">
    :   <dfn>handle</dfn>
    ::  The account's [=/handle=], in the canonical form that [=parse a handle=] produces. The user
        agent uses it to match the account with registered and typed [=/handle|handles=]
        ([=find matching accounts=]).
</dl>

Note: This is separate from {{IdentityProviderAccount/username}}, which an [=IDP=] can use for
any display name it likes. An account can have both.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>find matching accounts</dfn> given a [=list=] of {{IdentityProviderAccount}}s |accounts|
and a [=/handle=] |handle|, return the [=list/items=] of |accounts| whose
{{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}} [=map/exists=] and [=handles match|matches=] |handle|.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=fetch the accounts=], change "has neither {{IdentityProviderAccount/email}},
{{IdentityProviderAccount/name}}, {{IdentityProviderAccount/tel}}, nor
{{IdentityProviderAccount/username}} set" to also allow {{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}}.

</div>

## Declaring supported federations ## {#declaring-federations}

<xmp class="idl">
partial dictionary IdentityProviderAPIConfig {
  sequence<USVString> federations;
};
</xmp>

<dl dfn-type="dict-member" dfn-for="IdentityProviderAPIConfig">
    :   <dfn>federations</dfn>
    ::  The {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} URLs that the [=IDP=] supports. An [=IDP=] that
        doesn't list a federation is never returned for a [=federation request=] for that
        federation.
</dl>

<div class="example" id="example-declaring-federations">
An [=IDP=]'s [=config file=] at `https://idp.example/fedcm.json` declares that it supports both
IndieAuth and AT Protocol:

```json
{
  "accounts_endpoint": "/accounts",
  "id_assertion_endpoint": "/assertion",
  "login_url": "/login",
  "federations": [
    "https://www.w3.org/TR/indieauth/",
    "https://atproto.com"
  ]
}
```

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# User-initiated Registration # {#user-registration}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

A user whose [=/handle=] isn't in the [=handle registry=] yet can type it when signing in to an
[=RP=] (via the [=find your account affordance=] in [[#presentation]]). The user agent
[=look up a typed handle|resolves=] the typed handle, checks that its [=issuer=] supports the
requested federation, and adds the handle to the [=handle registry=] once the user selects it.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>find your account</dfn> given an {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} |federationRequest| and a
|globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns a pair (an {{IdentityProviderAccount}}, a
[=registered provider=]), or null.

1. Show UI that asks the user to type a handle. The user agent MAY offer suggestions from its own
    local data (such as the [=/handle|handles=] in the [=handle registry=]). It MUST NOT
    send network requests while the user types.
1. Wait for the user to submit a [=string=] |input|, or to cancel. If the user cancels, return
    null.
1. Let |provider| be the result of [=look up a typed handle=] with |input|, |federationRequest|, and
    |globalObject|.
1. If |provider| is failure, tell the user that no account provider was found for |input|, and go
    back to the first step.
1. Let |handle| be |provider|'s [=registered provider/handles=][0].
1. If |provider|'s [=registered provider/accounts=] is not empty, show each of them. Otherwise,
    show a [=handle chip=] for |handle| and |provider|.
1. Wait for the user to select one of them, or to cancel. If the user cancels, return null.
1. [=Add to the handle registry=] with |handle|.

    Note: Selecting the [=/handle=] has the same effect as agreeing to
    [=ask to save a handle|save it=] after {{IdentityProvider/register()}}. The choice is stored
    as soon as the user selects it, before sign-in, so that it's remembered even if sign-in fails
    or the user abandons it.
1. If the user selected an account |account|, return (|account|, |provider|).
1. Let |account| be the result of [=sign in with a handle=] with |handle|, |provider|, and
    |globalObject|.
1. If |account| is failure, go back to the first step.
1. Return (|account|, |provider|).

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>look up a typed handle</dfn> given a [=string=] |input|, an
{{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} |federationRequest|, and a |globalObject|, run the following steps.
This returns a [=registered provider=] or failure.

1. Let |handle| be the result of [=parse a handle=] with |input|. If |handle| is failure, return
    failure.
1. Let |configUrl| be the result of [=resolve a handle=] with |handle| and |globalObject|. If
    |configUrl| is failure, return failure.
1. Let |provider| be the result of [=create a registered provider=] with |configUrl| and
    |federationRequest|.
1. Let |config| be the result of [=fetch the config file=] with |provider| and |globalObject|.
    If |config| is failure, return failure.
1. If |config|.{{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/federations}} doesn't [=map/exist=] or doesn't
    [=list/contain=] |federationRequest|.{{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}}, return failure.
1. Set |provider|'s [=registered provider/config=] to |config|, its
    [=registered provider/handles=] to « |handle| », and its [=registered provider/accounts=] to
    the result of [=get the registered accounts=] with |config|, |provider|, « |handle| », and
    |globalObject|.
1. Return |provider|.

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Presentation # {#presentation}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

This section defines how the user agent presents registered [=IDPs=] to the user when an [=RP=]
calls `navigator.credentials.get()` with a {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}}.

## Requesting by federation ## {#request-by-federation}

<xmp class="idl">
partial dictionary IdentityProviderConfig {
  USVString federation;
};
</xmp>

<dl dfn-type="dict-member" dfn-for="IdentityProviderConfig">
    :   <dfn>federation</dfn>
    ::  A URL that points to the definition of a federation the [=RP=] accepts: the protocol or
        profile that its [=IDPs=] and [=RPs=] follow, for example
        `"https://www.w3.org/TR/indieauth/"` or `"https://atproto.com"`. The authority behind the
        URL defines the federation. When present, {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}} is ignored,
        and the entry stands for every registered [=IDP=] whose [=config file=] lists this
        federation.
</dl>

An {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} whose {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} [=map/exists=] is a
<dfn>federation request</dfn>.

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [[FEDCM]], remove the `required` keyword from the {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}} and
{{IdentityProviderConfig/clientId}} members of {{IdentityProviderConfig}}.

Every algorithm in [[FEDCM]] that reads {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}} from an
{{IdentityProviderConfig}} passed by the [=RP=] MUST first check that the member [=map/exists=],
and treat its absence as failure. The one exception is [=create an IdentityCredential=], as
modified below.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=create an IdentityCredential=], insert the following steps immediately after the step
"Let <var ignore>mediation</var> be <var ignore>options</var>'s {{CredentialRequestOptions/mediation}}":

1. Let |federationRequests| be the [=list/items=] of |providerList| that are [=federation requests=].
1. If |federationRequests|'s [=list/size=] is greater than 1, return (failure, true).
1. [=list/Remove=] the [=list/items=] of |federationRequests| from |providerList|.
1. Let |federationRequest| be |federationRequests|[0] if it exists, and null otherwise.
1. If |federationRequest| is not null:
    1. Let |registered| be the result of [=gather registered providers=] with |federationRequest|,
        |providerList|, and |globalObject|.
    1. [=list/Extend=] |providerList| with |registered|.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=fetch an identity assertion=], change the entry ("client_id", |provider|'s
{{IdentityProviderConfig/clientId}}) in the "Create a list" step to:

1. ("client_id", |clientId|), where |clientId| is |provider|'s
    {{IdentityProviderConfig/clientId}} if it [=map/exists=], and otherwise the
    [=ASCII serialization of an origin|serialization=] of |globalObject|'s
    [=associated Document=]'s [=Document/origin=].

</div>

Issue: Is the [=RP=]'s origin the right default `client_id` for registered [=IDPs=]? It matches
how IndieAuth identifies clients by URL, but other protocols may expect something else.

Note: {{IdentityCredential/configURL}} tells the [=RP=] which [=IDP=] issued the token, so it can
verify the token using the protocol named by {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}}.

## Registered providers ## {#registered-providers}

A <dfn>registered provider</dfn> is an {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} that the user agent
creates for the [=IDP=] of one or more [=/handle|handles=], rather than one the [=RP=] passed. The
user agent keeps the following associated with it:

<dl dfn-for="registered provider">
    :   <dfn>config</dfn>
    ::  The {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig}} from [=fetch the config file=].
    :   <dfn>handles</dfn>
    ::  A [=list=] of [=/handle|handles=] that [=resolve a handle|resolve=] to this [=IDP=]: the
        ones in the [=handle registry=], or the single [=/handle=] the user typed.
    :   <dfn>accounts</dfn>
    ::  A [=list=] of {{IdentityProviderAccount}}s: the [=get the registered accounts|accounts=]
        that [=handles match|match=] one of its [=registered provider/handles=].
    :   <dfn>associated handle</dfn>
    ::  The [=/handle=] the user chose to sign in with, or null. [=Show an IDP login dialog=] sends
        it as `login_hint`.
</dl>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>create a registered provider</dfn> given a [=/URL=] |configUrl| and an
{{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} or null |federationRequest|, run the following steps. This returns an
{{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}}.

1. Let |provider| be a new {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}}.
1. Set |provider|.{{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}} to the [=URL serializer|serialization=]
    of |configUrl|.
1. If |federationRequest| is not null, then for each member name |m| of «
    {{IdentityProviderConfig/clientId}}, {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/params}},
    {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/fields}}, {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/loginHint}},
    {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/domainHint}} »: if |federationRequest|[|m|] [=map/exists=], set
    |provider|[|m|] to |federationRequest|[|m|].
1. Mark |provider| as a [=registered provider=].
1. Return |provider|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>get the registered accounts</dfn> given an {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig}} |config|, an
{{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} |provider|, a [=list=] of [=/handle|handles=] |handles|, and a
|globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns a [=list=] of {{IdentityProviderAccount}}s.

1. Let |origin| be the [=url/origin=] of the result of running the [=URL parser=] on |provider|'s
    {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}}.
1. If [=get the login status=] for |origin| is not [=logged-in=], return an empty [=list=].
1. Let |accountsList| be the result of [=create identity credential/fetch accounts=] with |config|,
    |provider|, and |globalObject|.
1. Let |result| be an empty [=list=].
1. For each |handle| of |handles|: [=list/extend=] |result| with the [=list/items=] of the result
    of [=find matching accounts=] with |accountsList| and |handle| that |result| doesn't already
    [=list/contain=].
1. Return |result|.

</div>

Note: Only accounts whose {{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}} is a registered [=/handle=] are shown
to other [=RPs=], because those are the accounts the user agreed to (with
{{IdentityProvider/register()}}, or by typing the [=/handle=]). An [=IDP=]'s
{{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} can return other accounts (or push them via
[[ACCOUNT-REGISTRY]]), but they aren't shown on a [=federation request=] until their
[=/handle|handles=] are registered.

Issue: Should other accounts from a registered [=IDP=] also be shown, once the user has
registered at least one [=/handle=] with it?

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>group registered handles by config URL</dfn> given a |globalObject|, run the following steps.
This returns an [=ordered map=] from [=/URL|URLs=] of [=config files=] to [=lists=] of
[=/handle|handles=].

1. Let |groups| be an empty [=ordered map=].
1. For each |handle| of the [=handle registry=]:
    1. Let |configUrl| be the result of [=resolve a handle=] with |handle| and |globalObject|. If
        |configUrl| is failure, [=iteration/continue=].
    1. If |groups|[|configUrl|] doesn't [=map/exist=], [=map/set=] |groups|[|configUrl|] to an
        empty [=list=].
    1. [=list/Append=] |handle| to |groups|[|configUrl|].
1. Return |groups|.

</div>

Note: Several [=/handle|handles=] can resolve to the same [=IDP=], for example when two users of
the same [=IDP=] share a user agent. Grouping them means the user agent fetches that [=IDP=]'s
[=config file=] and accounts once.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>gather registered providers</dfn> given an {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}}
|federationRequest|, a [=list=] of {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} |explicitProviders|, and a
|globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns a [=list=] of [=registered providers=].

1. Let |federation| be |federationRequest|.{{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}}.
1. Let |seenOrigins| be the [=ordered set=] of the [=url/origins=] of the
    {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}}s of |explicitProviders|.
1. Let |result| be an empty [=list=].
1. For each |configUrl| → |handles| of the result of [=group registered handles by config URL=] with
    |globalObject|:
    1. Let |origin| be |configUrl|'s [=url/origin=].
    1. If |seenOrigins| [=list/contains=] |origin|, [=iteration/continue=].

        Note: If the [=RP=] also lists this [=IDP=] explicitly, the explicit entry is used and
        the registration is ignored, so that the same [=IDP=] doesn't appear twice.
    1. Let |provider| be the result of [=create a registered provider=] with |configUrl| and
        |federationRequest|.
    1. Let |config| be the result of [=fetch the config file=] with |provider| and |globalObject|.
    1. If |config| is failure, [=iteration/continue=].
    1. If |config|.{{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/federations}} doesn't [=map/exist=] or doesn't
        [=list/contain=] |federation|, [=iteration/continue=].
    1. Let |accounts| be the result of [=get the registered accounts=] with |config|, |provider|,
        |handles|, and |globalObject|.
    1. Set |provider|'s [=registered provider/config=] to |config|, its
        [=registered provider/handles=] to |handles|, and its [=registered provider/accounts=] to
        |accounts|.
    1. [=set/Append=] |origin| to |seenOrigins|, and [=list/append=] |provider| to |result|.
1. Return |result|.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=fetch the config file=], insert the following step immediately before the step "If <var ignore>rpOrigin</var>
is not an [=opaque origin=], and ...":

1. If |provider| is a [=registered provider=], set <var ignore>skipWellKnown</var> to true.

Note: The [=well-known file=] exists to stop an [=RP=] from encoding tracking information in the
config URL it passes. A [=registered provider=]'s config URL doesn't come from the [=RP=]. The user
agent obtained it from the [=well-known file=] itself, by [=resolve a handle|resolving=] a
[=/handle=].

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=create an IdentityCredential=], in the step "For each |provider| in |providerList|", insert the
following as the first sub-step:

1. If |provider| is a [=registered provider=]:
    1. Set |providerMap|[<var ignore>providerOrigin</var>] to |provider|'s [=registered provider/accounts=].
    1. [=iteration/Continue=].

    Note: For registered providers, [=gather registered providers=] has already fetched the
    [=config file=] and matching accounts. If no matching account is signed in, the user agent
    shows a [=handle chip=] instead of mismatch UI.

</div>

## Account selection ## {#account-selection}

A <dfn>handle chip</dfn> for a [=/handle=] |handle| and a [=registered provider=] |provider| is a
user agent UI element that represents an account the user hasn't signed in to yet:

* Its primary label is |handle|.
* It SHOULD show the [=federation branding=] for the {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} requested.
    If there isn't any, it uses a generic icon.
* It MAY show the [=url/host=] of |provider|'s {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}} as secondary
    text, so that the user knows where they'll sign in.

An account from [=find matching accounts=] is shown the same way FedCM shows accounts. The user
agent MAY add the [=federation branding=] as a badge.

<div algorithm>
The <dfn>federation branding</dfn> for a {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} |federation| is a name and an
icon published by the authority behind |federation|, or null. The user agent obtains it without
credentials and MAY cache it.

</div>

Issue(29): Define where the federation authority publishes its branding, and in which format.

<div algorithm="sign in with a handle">
To <dfn>sign in with a handle</dfn> given a [=/handle=] |handle|, a [=registered provider=]
|provider|, and a |globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns an
{{IdentityProviderAccount}} or failure.

1. Set |provider|'s [=registered provider/associated handle=] to |handle|.
1. Let |result| be the result of [=show an IDP login dialog=] with |provider|'s
    [=registered provider/config=], |provider|, and |globalObject|.
1. If |result| is failure, return failure.
1. Let |matches| be the result of [=get the registered accounts=] with |provider|'s
    [=registered provider/config=], |provider|, « |handle| », and |globalObject|.
1. If |matches|'s [=list/size=] is 1, return |matches|[0].
1. If |matches|'s [=list/size=] is greater than 1, let the user choose one of |matches| and
    return it. If the user cancels, return failure.
1. Tell the user that the account they signed in with doesn't match |handle|, and return failure.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=show an IDP login dialog=], insert the following step immediately after the step that
appends `login_hint`:

1. If |provider| is a [=registered provider=] with an
    [=registered provider/associated handle=] |handle|, and
    |provider|'s {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/loginHint}} is empty, [=list/append=]
    ("login_hint", |handle|) to <var ignore>queryList</var>.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=create an IdentityCredential=]:

Change the step "If |providerMap| [=map/is empty=], return (failure, false)" to:

1. If |providerMap| [=map/is empty=]:
    1. If |federationRequest| is null, or <var ignore>mode</var> is not
        {{IdentityCredentialRequestOptionsMode/active}}, return (failure, false).
    1. Otherwise, the user agent MAY continue, showing only the
        [=find your account affordance=].

In the "Build UI" step, add the following for each [=registered provider=] |provider| in
|providerList|:

1. For each [=/handle=] |handle| of |provider|'s [=registered provider/handles=] for which
    [=find matching accounts=] with |provider|'s [=registered provider/accounts=] and |handle|
    returns an empty [=list=], add a [=handle chip=] for |handle| and |provider|. If the user
    selects it:
    1. Let |account| be the result of [=sign in with a handle=] with |handle|, |provider|, and
        |globalObject|.
    1. If |account| is not failure, set |selectedAccount| to |account| and |permission| to true,
        set the relevant provider to |provider| and the relevant config to |provider|'s
        [=registered provider/config=], and close the dialog.

After the "Build UI" step, add the following step:

1. If |federationRequest| is not null, the user agent SHOULD add a
    <dfn>find your account affordance</dfn>. If the user triggers it:
    1. Let |result| be the result of [=find your account=] with |federationRequest| and |globalObject|.
    1. If |result| is not null, let (|account|, |resolvedProvider|) be |result|. Set
        |selectedAccount| to |account| and |permission| to true, set the relevant provider to
        |resolvedProvider| and the relevant config to |resolvedProvider|'s
        [=registered provider/config=], and close the dialog.

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Requirements on other parties # {#requirements}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

*This section is non-normative.*

<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th>Party<th>What it does
<tbody>
<tr><td>[=IDP=]<td>
    Lists the federations it supports in {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/federations}}.
    Serves a [=well-known file=] at its [=site of a host|site=] with a single
    {{IdentityProviderWellKnown/provider_urls}} entry. Sets each account's
    {{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}} to the user's handle in its
    {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} response (or when pushing accounts with
    [[ACCOUNT-REGISTRY]]). After the user signs in, sets its login status to [=logged-in=] and calls
    {{IdentityProvider/register()}} with that handle. Calls {{IdentityProvider/unregister()}} when
    the handle stops existing (e.g. the account is deleted or renamed), and
    `setStatus("logged-out")` when the user merely signs out. Accepts `login_hint` on its
    {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/login_url}}.
<tr><td>Owner of a handle's [=handle/site=]<td>
    Publishes a [[#dns-record|delegation record]] naming the [=issuer=].
<tr><td>Federation authority<td>
    Publishes [=federation branding=] for its {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}}.
<tr><td>[=RP=]<td>
    Passes a {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}}, and verifies the returned token according to that
    federation, including checking that it was issued for the handle and by the
    {{IdentityCredential/configURL}}'s [=IDP=].
</table>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Privacy considerations # {#privacy}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th>Request<th>When<th>Credentials<th>Reveals the RP?
<tbody>
<tr><td>[=Config file=] of registered [=IDPs=]<td>On a [=federation request=]<td>No<td>No
<tr><td>{{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} of registered [=IDPs=]<td>On a
    [=federation request=], when the [=IDP=]'s login status is [=logged-in=]<td>Yes<td>No
<tr><td>DNS `TXT` lookup and [=well-known file=], for each registered [=/handle=]<td>On a
    [=federation request=], unless cached<td>No<td>No
<tr><td>DNS `TXT` lookup, [=well-known file=], [=config file=], and (when [=logged-in=])
    {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} of the resolved [=IDP=]<td>After
    the user submits a handle, or when an [=IDP=] calls {{IdentityProvider/register()}} or
    {{IdentityProvider/unregister()}}<td>Only for
    {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}}<td>No
<tr><td>{{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/login_url}}<td>After the user selects a [=handle chip=]<td>Yes
    (a top-level navigation)<td>No
<tr><td>{{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/id_assertion_endpoint}}<td>After the user selects an
    account<td>Yes<td>Yes
</table>

* A registered [=IDP=] whose login status is [=logged-out=] receives no credentialed request
    before the user chooses to sign in with a [=handle chip=]. When the [=IDP=]'s login status is
    [=logged-in=], the user agent fetches its {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} as in
    [[FEDCM]] (unless the [=IDP=] and user agent use [[ACCOUNT-REGISTRY]], which avoids the
    credentialed fetch).
* Typing a handle reveals its [=handle/site=] to the DNS resolver and to the resolved [=IDP=]'s
    servers, and signing in with a [=handle chip=] sends the handle to the [=IDP=], but nothing is
    revealed to the [=RP=] until the user finishes signing in.
* Because the [=handle registry=] stores only [=/handle|handles=], a [=federation request=] can cause
    DNS lookups for the [=handle/sites=] of the user's registered [=/handle|handles=], which the DNS
    resolver can observe. The requests don't carry credentials or reveal the [=RP=]. User agents
    can reduce this by caching resolution results (see [[#registry]]).
* The [=handle registry=] is global. It is used only in user agent UI, and is never exposed to the
    [=RP=] until the user selects an account.
* The [=promise rejection delay|delay before rejection=] defined in [[FEDCM]] still applies, so an
    [=RP=] can't tell whether the user has any registered [=IDPs=].

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Security considerations # {#security}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

* **Resolution isn't proof.** An attacker who spoofs DNS, or controls a [=site of a host|site=]'s
    [=well-known file=], can make resolution end at an [=IDP=] of their choosing. That gets them the typed
    handle. They don't get the user's credentials for the real [=IDP=], and they can't make the
    [=RP=] accept a token for someone else's handle, as long as the [=RP=] verifies the token using
    its protocol (for example, atproto's two-way check between handle and DID). This is the same
    reasoning as the Email Verification Protocol's analysis of DNS delegation.
* **A site speaks for its subdomains.** A [=/handle=]'s [=handle/site=] is its
    [=host/registrable domain=], just as for cookies and the FedCM [=well-known file=]. Hosts that
    give independent users their own subdomains need to be on the Public Suffix List to keep them
    separate.
* **Handles that are mistyped or don't exist** still resolve to the provider. However,
    [=find matching accounts=] returns nothing, and the [=handle chip=] shows the handle exactly as
    typed.
* **An account that doesn't match** isn't used: [=sign in with a handle=] fails if the account the
    user signs in with doesn't match the handle.
* **Registration prompts** can be abused for spam. User agents can rate-limit
    {{IdentityProvider/register()}}, require [=transient activation=], and remember when the user
    declines.
* **A [=/handle=]'s [=issuer=] can change.** Because the [=handle registry=] stores only
    [=/handle|handles=], a registered [=/handle=] follows its [=handle/site=]: if the site's
    [[#dns-record|delegation record]] or the [=issuer=]'s [=well-known file=] changes, the
    [=/handle=] is offered with the new [=issuer=]. That is how a user moves a [=/handle=] to
    another [=IDP=], but it also means that whoever controls the site later (for example, after the
    domain expires) controls the handle's [=issuer=]. A user agent that caches resolution results
    MAY notice such a change and ask the user to confirm it.
* **An [=IDP=] can only register [=/handle|handles=] it is the [=issuer=] of.** {{IdentityProvider/register()}}
    requires the [=resolve a handle|resolved=] [=config file=] to be [=same site=] with the
    caller, so a site can't register handles whose [=issuer=] is another [=IDP=]. Similarly,
    {{IdentityProvider/unregister()}} only removes [=/handle|handles=] that resolve to the caller's
    [=site of a host|site=].

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Examples # {#examples}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

*This section is non-normative.*

<div class="example" id="example-same-site">
**The handle and the [=IDP=] are on the same site.** Alice runs her own atproto server at
`pds.alice.example`, and her handle is `@alice.example`.

1. Alice types `@alice.example` in [=find your account=]. [=Parse a handle=] gives
    `@alice.example`, whose [=handle/site=] is `alice.example`.
1. `_web-identity.alice.example` has `TXT "iss=alice.example"`, so [=resolve the issuer=] returns
    `alice.example`.
1. `https://alice.example/.well-known/web-identity` returns
    `{"provider_urls": ["https://pds.alice.example/config.json"]}`, which is on the same site.
1. The [=config file=] lists `https://atproto.com` in {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/federations}}.
1. Alice isn't signed in to `pds.alice.example` yet, so the user agent shows a [=handle chip=] for
    `@alice.example`.
1. When Alice selects it, the user agent adds `@alice.example` to the [=handle registry=] and opens
    `login_url?login_hint=%40alice.example`. Her server signs her in, its
    {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} returns an account with
    `{handle: "@alice.example"}`, and the user agent requests the token.

</div>

<div class="example" id="example-cross-site">
**The handle and the [=IDP=] are on different sites.** Bob's handle is `@bob.example`, and his
account is hosted by `social.example`.

1. Bob publishes `_web-identity.bob.example TXT "iss=social.example"`.
1. [=Resolve the issuer=] returns `social.example`. The user agent fetches
    `https://social.example/.well-known/web-identity` and then its [=config file=].
1. The user agent shows Bob's account with `handle: "@bob.example"` (if he is signed in to
    `social.example`), or a [=handle chip=].

Because `@bob.example` resolves to `social.example`, `social.example` can also call
`IdentityProvider.register("@bob.example")` after Bob signs in there. Another site, such as
`evil.example`, can't: the handle doesn't resolve to it.

</div>

<div class="example" id="example-subdomain">
**The handle is a subdomain of the provider.** Carol's handle is `@carol.social.example`.

1. Its [=handle/site=] is `social.example`, which publishes
    `_web-identity.social.example TXT "iss=social.example"`, so [=resolve the issuer=] returns
    `social.example`.
1. Resolution continues as in the previous example.

</div>

<div class="example" id="example-shared-host">
**Many users share a host.** Dave (`dave@social.example`) and Erin (`@erin.social.example`) both
resolve to the same [=config file=]. The [=handle registry=] has both [=/handle|handles=], and
[=group registered handles by config URL=] groups them together. On a [=federation request=], the
user agent fetches that [=config file=] and {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} once,
and shows the signed-in accounts that match `dave@social.example` or `@erin.social.example`, plus a
[=handle chip=] for either handle that doesn't have a matching signed-in account.

</div>

<div class="example" id="example-lifecycle">
**Signing out versus deleting an account.** Erin registered `@erin.social.example` from
`social.example`.

1. Erin signs out of `social.example`, which calls `navigator.login.setStatus("logged-out")`.
    `@erin.social.example` stays in the [=handle registry=]. On the next
    [=federation request=], the user agent sees that `social.example` is [=logged-out=] and shows a
    [=handle chip=] for `@erin.social.example`. Selecting it opens `social.example`'s
    {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/login_url}} with `login_hint=%40erin.social.example`.
1. Later, Erin deletes her account. `social.example` calls
    `IdentityProvider.unregister("@erin.social.example")`, and the [=/handle=] is removed from the
    [=handle registry=], so the handle is no longer offered to any [=RP=].

</div>

